<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tabaré Vázquez</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1940">1940</a>–<a href="/years/2020">2020</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tabaré Vázquez was a Uruguayan oncologist and politician of the Broad Front, the first left-wing president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a>. As mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> from 1990 to 1995 he built the coalition's base, and he served as president in 2005–2010 and 2015–2020. His governments expanded social spending and introduced strict tobacco-control laws.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tabare-vazquez.jpg" alt="Tabaré Vázquez" width="330" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Crespo · Attribution · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mariano_Rajoy_y_Tabaré_Vázquez_en_Palacio_de_La_Moncloa_2016_(5)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tabaré Ramón Vázquez Rosas was born in the La Teja neighbourhood of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> on 17 January 1940, the fourth child of Héctor Vázquez, a worker of the state company ANCAP, and Elena Rosas. His grandparents came from Ourense and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>. He studied medicine at the Medical School of the Universidad de la República and graduated as an oncologist in 1972. In 1976 a grant from the French government let him train further at the Gustave Roussy Institute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. He was president of the football club Club Progreso from 1979 to 1989.</p><p>From 1990 Vázquez was the first Intendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/broad-front-uruguay">Frente Amplio</a> coalition, carrying out the functions of both mayor of the city and governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. In 1994 he ran for president and received the most votes of any candidate, but under the Ley de Lemas system the election went to Julio María Sanguinetti of the Colorado Party, the highest-finishing candidate of the party with the most votes. In 1996 he replaced the coalition's historic leader, Líber Seregni, as its leader. In 1999, in the first election after the Ley de Lemas was scrapped, he led the first round with 40.1 percent but lost the runoff to Jorge Batlle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Vázquez won the presidency in the first round of the 2004 election, with 50.45 percent of valid votes, and took office on 1 March 2005. He was the first president from a left-wing party and the first since the 1830s who was not a member of the National or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a> parties. His first term was marked by a conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> over pulp mills on the Uruguay River and by closer relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and Argentina. He did not sign the Free Trade Area of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> proposed by George W. Bush. He strengthened the unions and improved social services; between 2005 and 2008 the minimum wage rose from 1,350 to 4,150 pesos. The constitution barred immediate re-election, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-mujica">José Mujica</a> succeeded him in 2010.</p><p>He was elected again in the runoff of 30 November 2014, defeating Luis Lacalle Pou, and took office on 1 March 2015. He devoted much of his second term to fighting non-communicable diseases. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-health-organization">World Health Organization</a> gave him its Director-General's Award in 2006 for his tobacco-control leadership.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Vázquez left office in March 2020. He died of lung cancer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> on 6 December 2020, aged 80, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> declared three days of national mourning. Because of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/covid-19-pandemic">COVID-19 pandemic</a> his funeral was held in intimacy, and he was buried at the Cementerio de La Teja beside his wife, María Auxiliadora Delgado, whom he had married in 1964.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tabaré Vázquez” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Since 2004 the left-wing Broad Front under <b>Tabaré Vázquez</b> and José Mujica has alternated with the National Party's Luis Alberto Lacalle Pou (2020), returning under Yamandú Orsi on 1 March 2025.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oriental-republic-of-uruguay">Oriental Republic of Uruguay · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/uruguay">Uruguay</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/uruguay">Uruguay</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-34.91,-56.19&amp;year=1992" aria-label="Montevideo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3125 3426 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tabaré Vázquez"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3575 3696h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3575 3696h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-34.91,-56.19&amp;year=1992">What was here in 1992? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
